 (need T9; 2 unseen).  \n- Characters: W3 only \u2013 hard to form sequences.  \n- Honors: J1 pair (valuable for Dragon Pung) + J3 single.  \n\nIf I aim for a standard 4-meld + pair hand, currently:  \n- Meld1: Exposed Chow B6-B8 (Dots).  \n- Need: Meld2, Meld3, Meld4 + pair.  \n\nPossible plan:  \n**Plan A**: Try for All Types + Dragon Pung + maybe Mixed Straight.  \nBut Mixed Straight needs specific chows in 3 suits, which is tough with only W3.  \n\n**Plan B**: Try for All Pungs + Dragon Pung(s) + All Types.  \nTo get All Pungs, I need all melds to be pungs/kongs. That means converting my sequences into pungs.  \n\nRight now, my tiles:  \n- J1 pair \u2192 possible pung if get another J1.  \n- T2 pair \u2192 possible pung if get another T2 (4 unseen).  \n- T3 pair \u2192 possible pung if get another T3 (4 unseen).  \n- Others are singles.  \n\nBut All Pungs requires four pungs; currently I have zero.  \n\n**Safer approach**: Discard isolated honors or tiles that don\u2019t help chows/pungs.  \n\nLooking at valid discards:  \n- W3 \u2013 only one, no other Characters in hand, hard to make chow/pung \u2192 weak tile.  \n- J3 \u2013 only one, no immediate pair, but J3 can pair if I draw another (2 unseen).  \n- T5, T7, T8 \u2013 these can form chows with other Bamboos if I draw T4, T6, T9 respectively.  \n- T2, T3 \u2013 have pairs, can become pungs or chows.  \n- J1 \u2013 have pair, potential pung.  \n\nBest discards are tiles that don\u2019t contribute to potential melds and are safe (not likely to give opponent pung/kong).  \n\nFrom game history:  \n- Opponents have discarded some terminals/honors: F4, T9, J2, etc.  \n- J1 was discarded by previous player once, so maybe safe. But J1 is valuable for my potential pung.  \n- W3 was discarded by next player once.  \n\nGiven my hand shape, keeping Bamboo tiles (T2,T3,T5,T7,T8) gives more flexibility for chows/pungs in one suit (Bamboo), and keeping J1 pair gives Dragon Pung chance.  \nWeakest tile currently: J3 (single honor, only 2 left in wall) or W3 (isolated in different suit).  \n\nBetween J3 and W3:  \n- J3: if I discard, I lose chance for Dragon Pung with J3, but I already have J1 pair for one Dragon Pung possibility.  \n- W3: if I discard, I lose any Character suit presence for All Types, but I can still get All Types if I have B, T, and honors (Dragons count as honors for All Types requirement? Yes, All Types requires all three suits + winds + dragons \u2192 but dragons are honors, so I just need each of the three suits somewhere. My exposed Chow is Dots, my hand has Bamboos, I need Characters somewhere. W3 alone doesn\u2019t help unless I form a meld with Characters. So discarding W3 might lose All Types unless I get another Character meld later.  \n\nGiven All Types is 6 points and achievable if I get a Character meld, but currently no other Characters, hard. Maybe abandon All Types aim.  \n\nThen safest discard: J3 (single honor, less useful than W3 which could combine with W1/W2 if drawn, but W1/W2 are also available). Actually, W3 can form Chow W1-W2-W3 if I draw W1 and W2 (3 W1 unseen, 4 W2 unseen). That\u2019s possible.  \n\nBut given I already have many Bamboo tiles, focusing on Bamboo chows/pungs + honors might be better.  \n\n**Immediate decision**:  \nKeep Bamboo tiles and J1 pair, discard isolated tile.  \nBetween W3 and J3:  \n- W3: can form chow with W1-W2.  \n- J3: can form pair/pung if drawn another.  \nBoth are similarly weak, but W3 might have slightly more potential because there are more W1/W2 unseen. However, my hand already has many Bamboo tiles; adding a Character chow would complicate hand shape.  \n\nSo discard J3 to keep hand more suited-focused.  \n\n**Final choice**: Play J3.  \n\nReason: J3 is a single honor with less chance to complete a dragon pung (only 2 left unseen), and it doesn\u2019t help sequences. Discarding it keeps my Bamboo concentration and preserves W3 which could still be used if I draw into Characters later, but even if not, it can be discarded later. It\u2019s a safe discard given opponents have discarded J3 before.  \n\n**Answer**: Play J3.  \n\n---\n\nEvaluation: I will discard J3 to keep hand flexibility and avoid discarding Bamboo tiles that can form chows/pungs.  \nReason: J3 is a single honor with low probability to complete a meld, and discarding it reduces honor clutter while maintaining potential for Bamboo sequences and J1 pung.  \nAnswer: Play J3."}
